From 0c4150e4ab48e5af7b85caeb2b5c6c3d244f89d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chromium Extend <noreply@localhost>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 03:01:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 49/49] Add a Video autostart site setting

Settings, Site settings, Video autostart. Allowed keeps whatever autoplay behaviour the
browser already had, so leaving it alone changes nothing. Blocked means nothing plays until
the user presses play.

Chromium already registers ContentSettingsType::AUTOPLAY and stores it per site, and the
Android site settings framework already knows how to show a binary category. What was missing
is that nothing ever read the setting: the autoplay policy came from the command line alone,
so the setting existed and did nothing. This wires the two together and adds the category to
the site settings list.

The setting is applied after DetermineWebContentsAutoplayPolicy rather than before it. That
function has several paths which return kNoUserGestureRequired outright, for enterprise
policy, delegated permissions policy, microphone or camera access, and trusted web
activities, and each discards the policy it was given. Applying the setting first meant it
was computed correctly and then thrown away, which is exactly what happened during
development: the browser had worked out kUserGestureRequired and the renderer still received
the default. Someone who has turned autostart off should not be overruled because they once
allowed a microphone.

Verified with a page that calls play() from a timer eight seconds after a tap, which is past
the five second transient activation window, so the two policies can be told apart. Blocked
rejects it and allowed does not.
